  "explanation": {
    "short": "Five vowel letters are written before the consonant but said after it: เ แ โ ไ ใ. So ไป is p + ai (\"pai\"), and แม่ is m + ae (\"mae\").",
    "more": "ไ and ใ are the same sound (\"ai\", like \"eye\"), and there are only about twenty words with ใ, so you learn those by heart; everything else with this sound is ไ. เ is \"ay\", and with a ็ above it or a closing sound it turns short (เด็ก is \"dek\", เป็น is \"pen\"), แ is \"ae\" as in \"cat\", โ is \"oh\". Your eye jumps ahead to the consonant first, then back to the vowel. The tone still comes from the first consonant: ไม่ is a low-class ม with ่, so it falls."
  },
